WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.750
In this module we'll be talking about

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:03.630
memory. Because as you start using Cloud

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:05.470
Code, especially skills which let you

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:07.710
use Cloud Code much faster, it actually

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:09.630
starts to build up your context. And by

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:11.510
context we basically just mean all of

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.870
the files that get added or created in

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:15.870
your workspace folder. And if you don't

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:18.190
understand how memory works, then that

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.470
pile of files, which grows the more you

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:22.910
work, will slowly but surely eat up your

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:25.230
tokens and usage limits unless you know

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:27.230
how to manage memory well. And if you

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.190
need a quick visual of how memory works,

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:30.950
it's really really simple. You can think

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:32.749
of it as a file cabinet that your

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:34.670
digital employee, your AI agent in this

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:36.950
case, has access to. So everything that

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.630
you have built and all of the context

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.710
that you work with your agents with get

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:42.550
filed in this cabinet. And if you have

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.470
your memory set up correctly, then Cloud

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:46.510
Code will be able to retrieve the right

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:48.150
folder in the moment that it is

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:49.950
relevant. And broadly, there are just

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:52.350
two important files and two levels of

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:54.750
memory that you need to be aware of and

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:56.710
always be conscious of. The first one is

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:58.390
going to be sort of your employee

00:00:58.400 --> 00:00:59.470
handbook, which is going to be the

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:02.190
claw.md. And the bigger drawer, which as

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.150
the agent's longer-term memory, is his

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:06.470
memory.md, which is going to be the rest

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.430
of the file cabinet in our analogy. So

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:10.350
we'll tackle these two in turn so that

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:11.950
you have a clear understanding of how

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:14.190
memory works and how it can best fit

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:16.880
your setup.
